'' AN AGGREGATE LOAD CARRIES ITS SIZE, AND TWO SHAPES HAD NONE.
''
'' MIR keeps an aggregate's width beside the value rather than in its type, and the
'' emitter recovers it from the address's SLOT when the instruction does not say. A
'' deref and a pointer subscript both produce a computed TEMP -- there is no slot --
'' so both wrote '[0 x i8]':
''
''     %t65 = load [0 x i8], ptr %t64
''     store [1 x i8] %t65, ptr %s91
''
'' clang refused the module: no object for
'' tests/vendor/fbc-tests/functions/odd-arg.bas, which passes an odd-sized UDT four
'' ways. '*p' takes its size from the node's own type; 'p[j]' from the target's
'' ELEMENT type, because Check does not type an N_INDEX at all.
''
'' ALL FOUR ARGUMENT SHAPES ARE HERE -- a local, a deref, an array element and a
'' pointer subscript -- because only two of them were wrong and a test of one would
'' have passed before the fix.
''
'' Sizes 1, 2, 3, 5, 7 and 8: the odd ones are the point. Differenced against fbc,
'' which prints the same '1 2 3 5 7 8' and the same silence from the checks.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/odd-sized-udt-arg.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' NO ZERO-WIDTH AGGREGATE ANYWHERE. This is the assertion that failed before the
'' fix, and it is the one that stays true whatever the slot numbering does.
'' EXPECT-NOT: [0 x i8]
'' GOLDEN: expected/odd-sized-udt-arg.out
